          <a href="#skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. When Not to Buy</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Buy a 42-inch zero turn when something narrow has to get through: a fence gate, a side yard, a shed door. For one to two acres of grass with trees and beds, it mows nearly as fast as a wider machine and leaves less trimming. The catch is that a 42-inch mower is not 42 inches wide, and the decks in this class range from thin stamped steel to welded 10-gauge plate.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-42" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why 42 Inches Is the Right Size for So Many Lawns
          </h2>

          <p>
            Forty-two inches is the narrowest deck most manufacturers put on a full-size residential zero turn, and for a large share of suburban lots it is the correct size rather than a compromise. The reason is that the deck has to get to the grass before it can cut it. Every gate, the gap between the shed and the fence, the side yard between two houses, the door of the garage or shed where it lives, and the ramp of the trailer it rides on all set a hard limit on width, and a 42-inch machine clears far more of those limits than a 48 or 54-inch one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck width is also not the whole story on mowing time. A residential zero turn runs at 6.5 to 8 mph on open ground according to the spec sheets on this page, against roughly 3 to 5 mph for a typical lawn tractor, so a 42-inch zero turn covers open ground about as fast as a much wider tractor. On a lawn broken up by trees, beds and play equipment, a narrower machine also loses less time to turning and leaves less for the string trimmer. That is why the pillar <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower/">zero turn mower guide</a> puts 42 inches at up to about an acre, and the manufacturers here rate their 42-inch machines for up to 2 acres.
          </p>

          <p>
            The size stops making sense on open ground. Two or more acres of open, straight-run grass is where a 48 to 54-inch deck saves real minutes every week, and on that kind of property the extra width costs nothing at the gate because there usually is no gate. If that describes your lot, the <a href="/best-residential-zero-turn-mower/">best residential zero turn mowers</a> guide covers the 48 to 54-inch class. This page is for the lot where something narrow has to get through.
          </p>

