        <div id="gate-width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Measure the Gate, Then Read the Overall Width
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common 42-inch buying mistake is assuming a 42-inch mower is 42 inches wide. It is not. The cutting width is the blade circle; the machine is wider because of the deck housing, the discharge chute and the anti-scalp wheels. Husqvarna is unusually clear about this on the Z242F Special Edition, listing an overall width of 48 inches with the chute folded up and 56 inches with it down. That is the realistic shape of the class: about 6 inches over the cutting width with the chute up, and about 14 inches over with it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the number to compare with a gate is the chute-up width, plus clearance for your hands on the lap bars and for not scraping the gate post. A standard 48-inch garden gate is too tight for most 42-inch zero turns even with the chute up; a 60-inch gate is comfortable. If your opening is somewhere in between, ask the dealer for the overall width with the chute raised, or measure the floor model with a tape before you sign. Every chute on the gas machines here folds up for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage is the second width problem. A one-car garage bay is about 9 to 10 feet wide, and a 42-inch zero turn parked against one wall leaves room for a car; a 54-inch machine often does not. A typical 8x10 garden shed with a 4-foot double door is borderline for 42 inches and hopeless for anything wider. Measure the door, not the shed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Chute-up width plus 4 inches must clear the narrowest gap</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For a 42-inch zero turn that usually means an opening of at least 52 inches. Measure every gate and door on the route before you choose a deck size, and if the spec sheet only gives cutting width, ask for overall width with the chute raised.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Trailers matter too if you will haul the mower. Most 5x8 utility trailers have about 60 inches between the fenders, which takes a 42-inch zero turn easily. Weight is the other check: the gas machines here weigh roughly 530 to 560 lb on the spec sheets that publish it, which most light utility trailers and folding ramps rated at 1,000 lb or more handle without trouble.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Stamped vs Fabricated Decks at 42 Inches
          </h2>

          <p>
            At this size, the deck is where the real difference between a $3,200 machine and a $3,600 machine lives. A stamped deck is pressed from a single sheet of steel; a fabricated deck is welded from thicker plate. Gauge numbers run backwards, so lower is thicker: 13-gauge is about 0.09 inches, 11-gauge about 0.12 inches and 10-gauge about 0.135 inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three of the six picks use stamped decks: the Toro TimeCutter and Troy-Bilt Mustang Z42 at 13-gauge, and the EGO at 12-gauge. Two use fabricated decks: the Cub Cadet Ultima Z2 42 at 11-gauge and the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition at 10-gauge. Deere lists the Z315E's 42-inch Accel Deep deck by name but not by gauge on its own spec page.
          </p>

          <p>
            A stamped deck cuts perfectly well. Where it loses is in abuse: hitting a buried root, a stump edge or a landscape timber at speed can dent or crack a thin stamped shell, and rust starts at the welds of the deck hangers and the spindle mounts. On a smooth, mature suburban lawn a stamped deck will usually outlast the engine. On a rough lot with roots, a fence line and an old orchard, the fabricated deck is the part that earns its price.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other deck spec to check is the spindle. Husqvarna specifies cast-iron spindle housings with greasable mandrels on the Z242F; greasable spindles let you push fresh grease through each season, which is the cheapest insurance a mower owner can buy. Sealed spindles are maintenance-free until the bearing goes, then they are a part to replace. Owners commonly report that spindle bearings are the first deck repair on a residential mower, so a greasable design is worth having at any price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transaxles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Engines and Transaxles: What the Spec Sheet Tells You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Engines across this class sit between 20 and 22.5 hp, which is plenty for a 42-inch deck. The gas engines here are all V-twins or twin-cylinder designs from Toro, Kawasaki, Kohler and Deere's own branded line. Kawasaki's FR series, on the Cub Cadet and Husqvarna, has a strong reputation among owners for longevity in residential mowers, and Husqvarna lists a full-pressure lubrication system with an oil filter on the Z242F, which is worth having on any engine you expect to run for ten seasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            Horsepower differences of two or three show up mostly in thick, wet spring growth and when bagging. If you plan to bag clippings on a 42-inch machine, see our <a href="/best-zero-turn-mower-with-bagger/">zero turn mowers with baggers</a> guide; the extra blower load is where a 20 hp engine starts to feel stretched.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transaxles are the more important spec. Every zero turn steers by driving each rear wheel with its own hydrostatic transaxle, and those units are the most expensive common failure on a residential mower. The Cub Cadet and Troy-Bilt use the Hydro-Gear EZT 2200, a sealed, maintenance-free unit. Toro steps up to the Hydro-Gear ZT2100 on the TimeCutter. Husqvarna and Deere specify hydrostatic drive without naming the unit on the pages we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a one to two acre lawn mowed 25 to 30 times a year, a sealed transaxle is adequate. The units that let you change fluid and filters, such as the ZT-3100 and up, belong on 48-inch-plus prosumer machines and are covered in the pillar guide. Do not overpay for transaxle capacity a 42-inch lawn will not use; do avoid running a sealed unit hot by towing heavy loads up hills, which is the fastest way to wear one out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is an Electric 42-Inch Zero Turn Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Forty-two inches is the sweet spot for battery zero turns because it is the deck size a battery pack can carry across a realistic suburban lawn. EGO rates the Z6 e-Steer for up to 2 acres per charge on its six-battery kit, which covers the same ground as the gas machines here on a good day. The real-world figure depends on grass height, moisture and whether you mulch or bag, and owners commonly report less than the headline in long, wet grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for electric is not cost. The EGO costs a great deal more than any gas 42-inch machine on this list. The case is everything else: no oil changes, no spark plugs, no carburetor to gum up over winter, no fuel stabilizer, and a mower you can run early on a Sunday without the neighbors noticing. The e-Steer model adds a steering wheel and cruise control, which makes it the easiest machine here for someone who has never used lap bars.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case against is the charge. A gas mower that runs low takes two minutes to refill; a battery mower that runs out at three-quarters of the lawn is done for the next few hours. If your lawn is close to the rated acreage, a battery mower is a gamble on growth and weather every week. Our <a href="/best-electric-zero-turn-mower/">electric zero turn mower guide</a> compares the full battery class, including lap-bar and larger-deck models, if you are leaning that way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slopes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Slopes, Steering and the Lap-Bar Learning Curve
          </h2>

          <p>
            Zero turns are worse on slopes than lawn tractors, and a narrow 42-inch machine does not change that. Steering comes from the drive wheels, so when the downhill wheel loses grip on wet grass you lose steering and braking together. Manufacturers commonly limit residential zero turns to around 15 degrees in their manuals; check the exact limit in the manual for the machine you buy and treat it as a maximum, not a target.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your lawn has a real side hill, a ditch bank or a pond edge, a lawn tractor with front-wheel steering is the safer tool, and our guides to the <a href="/best-lawn-tractor/">best lawn tractors</a> and <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">mowers for hills</a> cover that case. The <a href="/zero-turn-vs-riding-mower/">zero turn vs riding mower comparison</a> goes through the trade-off in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            On level and gently rolling ground, the lap bars take most new owners one or two mowings to learn. Push both bars forward to go straight, pull one back to turn, and keep your movements small. The EGO e-Steer sidesteps the learning curve with a steering wheel, which is worth considering if several people in the household will mow. Every gas machine here uses lap bars.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What a 42-Inch Zero Turn Costs to Own
          </h2>

          <p>
            List prices for the gas machines here run from $3,229 for the Troy-Bilt Mustang Z42 to $3,599 for the Cub Cadet Ultima Z2 42, per the manufacturers' published MSRPs, with the Husqvarna at $3,499. Dealers and big-box stores run promotions through the year, and financing offers are common, so the out-the-door price is often below list in the off-season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running costs are modest. Expect an oil and filter change, an air filter, a spark plug pair and a blade sharpening each season, and a set of new blades every season or two depending on how sandy your ground is. Our <a href="/riding-lawn-mower-maintenance/">riding mower maintenance guide</a> and <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/">blade sharpening guide</a> cover the schedule. The large, unpredictable costs are transaxles and spindles, which is why the deck and drive specs in this guide matter more than the engine badge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty terms differ more than the brochures suggest. Toro gives 3 years with unlimited hours residential, and Cub Cadet 3 years unlimited hours with a lifetime limited frame and deck shell warranty. Deere's is 3 years or 200 hours bumper-to-bumper. EGO's mower warranty is 5 years or 300 hours. For a one-acre lawn mowed weekly you will put on roughly 20 to 30 hours a year, so an hour cap is rarely the limit, but the frame and deck coverage on the Cub Cadet is a real point in its favor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When Not to Buy a 42-Inch Zero Turn
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip the category if your lawn is under half an acre. A quality self-propelled walk mower will do a quarter acre in about the time it takes to get a rider out of the shed, at a fraction of the price, and it fits through any gate. A small rear-engine rider or a 30-inch electric rider is a middle step if walking is the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if your lot is mostly open and over two acres. You will spend more of each summer on the seat than you need to, and a 48 to 54-inch deck pays for its extra cost in time within a couple of seasons. Skip it, too, if the property is hilly; a 42-inch deck on a zero turn is no safer on a slope than a 54-inch one.
          </p>

          <p>
            And do not buy a 42-inch zero turn expecting to tow a heavy cart up a hill or pull a dethatcher across the whole lawn every week. They have hitches, and light towing on level ground is fine, but sealed transaxles were not designed for repeated heavy pulling. If hauling is half the job, a garden tractor is the better machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the gate against the cutting width</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 42-inch deck is not a 42-inch machine. With the chute down, 42-inch zero turns commonly run around 56 inches wide, and even chute-up they are near 48. Measure every gate and door on the route and compare with the overall width the manufacturer lists, chute raised, plus a few inches of clearance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a stamped deck for rough ground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A stamped 13-gauge deck is fine on a smooth lawn and the first thing to suffer on roots, stumps and fence lines. If your lot has those, the fabricated decks on the Cub Cadet Ultima Z2 42 and Husqvarna Z242F are worth the extra few hundred dollars.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing from the deed instead of the grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lot size includes the house, the driveway and the beds. Trace the grass you actually mow on a satellite map. Many one-acre lots have two-thirds of an acre of turf, which is squarely 42-inch territory, and many buyers end up one deck size wider than they need.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing slopes the manual does not allow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A zero turn steers with its drive wheels, so losing traction on a wet side hill means losing steering and braking at once. Read the slope limit in the manual before you mow, and use a tractor or a walk mower on anything steeper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting an electric mower's rated acreage every time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturer runtime figures assume good conditions. Tall, wet spring grass and bagging both cut runtime noticeably, so if your lawn sits close to the rated acreage, expect some weeks where the battery runs out before the grass does.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 42 inch zero turn mower big enough for 2 acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be. Toro and John Deere both rate their 42-inch machines to mow up to 2 acres, and at 6.5 to 7 mph a 42-inch zero turn covers ground quickly. On open, simple ground over about 1.5 acres a 48 to 54-inch deck saves real time each week; on a lot with gates and obstacles the 42-inch machine is usually the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 42 inch zero turn fit through a 48 inch gate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not comfortably. Husqvarna lists the Z242F at 48 inches wide with the chute up and 56 inches with it down, which is typical of the class. Plan on an opening of at least 52 inches with the chute raised, and check the specific machine's overall width before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best small zero turn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most buyers, the Toro TimeCutter 42 (75742): a 22.5 hp V-twin, Hydro-Gear ZT2100 transaxles and Smart Speed for tight lawns, with an unlimited-hour residential warranty. If rough ground is the issue, the fabricated-deck Cub Cadet Ultima Z2 42 or Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 42 inch zero turn last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With seasonal oil changes, greased spindles and sharp blades, owners commonly report 10 or more seasons from a residential 42-inch zero turn on a one to two acre lawn. Transaxles and spindle bearings are the usual wear items, so a fabricated deck, greasable spindles and gentle use on hills extend life the most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 42 inch zero turn better than a 42 inch lawn tractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a flat or gently rolling lawn with trees and beds, yes: a zero turn is faster and leaves much less trimming. A lawn tractor is better on slopes, for towing and for owners who never want to learn lap bars. Our zero turn vs riding mower comparison covers the trade-off in detail.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are electric 42 inch zero turns as good as gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cut quality and speed on a lawn within their rated acreage, they are competitive. EGO rates the Z6 e-Steer for up to 2 acres per charge. They cost considerably more, and when the battery runs out the mowing stops until it recharges, so they suit lawns comfortably inside the rated range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do 42 inch zero turns come with a fabricated deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A few do. Of the machines on this list, the Cub Cadet Ultima Z2 42 has a fabricated 11-gauge deck and the Husqvarna Z242F Special Edition a fabricated 10-gauge deck. Most other 42-inch models, including the Toro TimeCutter and Troy-Bilt Mustang Z42, use stamped decks.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
